    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Assessment of the Offer\n\nYour offer is a **standard-to-solid initial offer** for an L6 PM at Google, but **not yet compelling enough to accept as-is** given that you are happy at Meta and were passively recruited.\n\n#### The Math: Google vs. Meta\n\nAt first glance, Google\u2019s 4-year annualized compensation looks like:\n* **Base:** $235,000\n* **Target Bonus (20%):** $47,000\n* **Equity (Annual Average):** $270,000 ($1.08M / 4)\n* **Annualized Total Compensation (TC):** **~$552,000** (vs. your current Meta run-rate of **$526,000**)\n\nHowever, **Google does not vest new-hire equity equally (25% per year)**. Google uses a **front-loaded schedule** (typically 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10% vesting monthly):\n\n| Year | Base + Bonus | Google RSUs (33/33/22/12) | Estimated Total Pay | Meta Baseline |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $282,000 | ~$356,400 (33%) | **~$638,400** | ~$476,000\u2013$526,000 |\n| **Year 2** | $282,000 | ~$356,400 (33%) | **~$638,400** | ~$526,000 |\n| **Year 3** | $282,000 | ~$237,600 (22%) | **~$519,600** | ~$526,000+ |\n| **Year 4** | $282,000 | ~$129,600 (12%) | **~$411,600** (Cliff) | ~$526,000+ |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **The Upside:** Years 1 and 2 deliver a significant pay bump over Meta (~+$110k/yr).\n2. **The Risk (The Year 3/4 Cliff):** Google\u2019s model relies on refreshers to fill the gap in Years 3 and 4. Because refreshers vest evenly over 4 years (25%/yr) and depend entirely on performance ratings, your compensation will drop in Years 3 and 4 unless you negotiate a larger initial grant or receive top-tier reviews.\n3. **The Gaps:** Your base salary would actually *decrease* by $5,000, and they offered **$0 sign-on bonus**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommendation: **Negotiate**\n\nDo not walk away, and do not accept yet. As a passive, high-performing Meta IC6 who is content staying put, **you have maximum leverage**. Google reached out to you, passed you through an L6 hiring committee, and wants to close you.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nAim to push the annualized run-rate toward **~$600k+** while securing upfront cash to bridge your move.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Your Counter Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | The posted band tops out at $271,000. It is completely reasonable to request that Google beat your current $240,000 base. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Standard tool Google uses to bridge unvested equity from Meta and incentivize passive talent to move. |\n| **New-Hire RSUs** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | A ~$1.28M grant (~$320k/yr avg) prevents the Year 3/4 cliff and raises Year 1 equity to ~$420k+. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Negotiation (Talking Points)\n\nSchedule a call with the recruiter and use the following positioning:\n\n1. **Express enthusiasm for the team:** Reiterate that you were impressed by the interviewers, the scope of the L6 role, and the product vision.\n2. **Anchor on your current situation:**\n   > *\"As I mentioned at the start of the process, I wasn't looking to move and am in a great spot at Meta making around $526,000. When I look at the numbers, the base salary is slightly below what I currently earn ($240k), and with no sign-on bonus, the transition carries some financial friction.\"*\n3. **Address the front-loading / cliff:**\n   > *\"I appreciate the front-loaded structure in Years 1 and 2, but looking at the 4-year annualized picture and the drop in Years 3 and 4, the total compensation delta isn't quite large enough to justify leaving my current trajectory and unvested equity.\"*\n4. **Make the specific ask:**\n   > *\"If we can get the **base salary to $255,000**, increase the **equity grant to $1,250,000**, and include a **$75,000 sign-on bonus**, this becomes an easy decision for me and I would be ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Decision Framework\n\n* **If Google meets you near the target** (e.g., ~$250k base, $1.2M+ equity, and a $50k\u2013$75k sign-on): **Accept.** You will earn $680k+ in Year 1 and establish a strong baseline.\n* **If Google refuses to move at all:** **Walk away.** A lateral move from Meta IC6 to Google L6 with a lower base salary and no sign-on bonus is not worth leaving a team where you are established and happy.",
