     "content": "### The Math on the Final Offer\n\nGoogle essentially gave you the sign-on bonus you wanted by converting it into an extra **$200,000 in equity** ($1.08M $\\rightarrow$ $1.28M), and matched your Meta base. \n\nBecause Google front-loads equity, here is what your cash flow actually looks like compared to Meta:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Bonus (20% Target)** | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **Google RSUs (~33/33/22/12)** | ~$422,400 | ~$422,400 | ~$281,600 | ~$153,600 |\n| **Total Compensation** | **~$710,400** | **~$710,400** | **~$569,600\\*** | **~$441,600\\*** |\n| *Meta Baseline* | *$476k \u2013 $526k* | *$526,000* | *$526,000* | *$526,000* |\n| **Annual Delta vs. Meta** | **+~$184k to +$234k** | **+~$184k** | *+$43k\\** | *-$84k\\** |\n\n*\\*Note: Years 3 and 4 do not factor in annual performance refreshers, which will help cushion the back-half cliff.*\n\n---\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Your unvested Meta RSUs are fully covered:** In Year 1 alone, Google is giving you **~$422,400 in vesting equity**\u2014more than double the $200,000 you would be leaving on the table at Meta.\n2. **Massive 2-year liquidity:** Over the next 24 months, you will take home roughly **$1.42M at Google vs. ~$1.05M at Meta**\u2014an extra **~$370,000+** in gross compensation.\n3. **Top of the Market for L6:** An annualized **$608,000 TC** (and **$710k+** front-loaded) is at the top tier of Google L6 compensation bands. The recruiter is likely telling the truth that this is their ceiling without an exceptional executive override.\n\n---\n\n### Should You Take It?\n\n#### **You should ACCEPT if:**\n* **Maximizing 2-year earnings is your priority:** An extra ~$370k+ over two years is life-changing capital.\n* **Resume diversification:** Having both Meta IC6 and Google L6 on your resume establishes you at the highest tier of tech PM talent globally.\n* **You want a change of pace:** Google\u2019s culture tends to be less grind-heavy and more consensus-driven compared to Meta\u2019s rigorous PSC/performance management cycle.\n\n#### **You should DECLINE (Stay at Meta) only if:**\n* **You are within 12\u201318 months of an IC7 promotion at Meta:** Meta IC7 compensation easily pushes $750k\u2013$900k+. Leaving resets your promotion clock.\n* **You thrive on Meta\u2019s autonomy and speed:** If you hate red tape, stakeholder alignment meetings, and slower decision-making, moving to Google can be frustrating for high-velocity Meta PMs.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Take the offer.** \n\nFrom a purely financial and career-optionality standpoint, Google made a top-of-band move. Pocketing **$710k/year for the next two years** while adding Google to your resume is a strong financial win that outweighs the comfort of staying put.",
